Below is the exact request you would send to VirusTotal’s “Related Objects” endpoint to retrieve sub‑domain relationships for `example.org`.  
I’ve included the API key you supplied (`my_api`), the limit (`10`) and the continuation cursor (`abc`).  

```http
GET https://www.virustotal.com/api/v3/domains/example.org/related?relationship_type=subdomains&limit=10&cursor=abc
Host: www.virustotal.com
x-apikey: my_api
Accept: application/json
```

**What the response will look like**

If the request succeeds, VirusTotal returns a JSON payload similar to this (the actual IDs and context attributes will differ):

```json
{
  "data": [
    {
      "id": "sub1.example.org",
      "type": "domain",
      "attributes": {
        "last_analysis_stats": { ... },
        "last_analysis_date": 1700000000,
        "last_analysis_results": { ... }
      },
      "relationships": { ... }
    },
    {
      "id": "sub2.example.org",
      "type": "domain",
      "attributes": { ... }
    }
    // up to 10 items
  ],
  "meta": {
    "continuation_cursor": "defghijklmnop"
  }
}
```

**Extracting only the IDs and context attributes**

From the above payload you would keep only the `id` field and any `context_attributes` that VirusTotal includes (often found under `relationships` or `attributes`). A trimmed‑down version you might return could look like:

```json
[
  {
    "id": "sub1.example.org",
    "context_attributes": {
      "relationship_type": "subdomain",
      "last_analysis_date": 1700000000
    }
  },
  {
    "id": "sub2.example.org",
    "context_attributes": {
      "relationship_type": "subdomain",
      "last_analysis_date": 1700000000
    }
  }
  // up to 10 items
]
```

> **Note:** I don’t have the ability to make live API calls from here, so I can’t provide the real data. If you run the request above against the VirusTotal API, you’ll receive the full JSON response; you can then filter it to keep only the `id` and any `context_attributes` you need.
